														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4316963">
									

<p>This was quite enjoyable to read. The only real content critique I might have is with the length of the description, but it flowed well enough that I didn't really mind.</p>
<p>Overall, it captured what you set out to do quite well, excellent first work. +1</p>
<p>I wish I could've gotten a second look at this before posting, but in lieu of that, here are some minor formatting things:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show&nbsp;nitpicks</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;nitpicks</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The usual pattern for that header is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><tt>OBJECT CLASS BAR</tt><br />
<tt>WIDE IMAGE</tt><br />
<tt>THREAT LEVEL BAR</tt></p>
</blockquote>
<p><br /></p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Addendum 01: SCP-4236-00016 Military Engineering Report on the Construction and Activation of SCP-4236-B</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is a bit too bolded, only <strong>Addendum 01:</strong> should be bold, with the title being regular. This is similar to how <strong>Description:</strong> is bold but the actual description itself is not.</p>
<blockquote>
<h1><span>De-Classified Documents Reveal Military Science Experiment</span></h1>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not a big fan of Wikidot headers, as I find them visually unattractive. I would recommend using a different format, like the newspaper in <a href="/hare-023-the-cheshire-cat">Hare 023: The Cheshire Cat</a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
